THOUGHT: Let me look more carefully at the Elements function and the Each method to see if there's a logical issue.

The Elements function:
```go
func Elements[T comparable](s Set[T]) func(func(element T) bool) {
	return func(yield func(element T) bool) {
		s.Each(func(t T) bool {
			return !yield(t)
		})
	}
}
```

The Each method:
```go
func (s *threadUnsafeSet[T]) Each(cb func(T) bool) {
	for elem := range *s {
		if cb(elem) {
			break
		}
	}
}
```

When Each calls cb(elem), if cb returns true, Each breaks (stops iteration).
In Elements, cb is the anonymous function that calls yield and returns !yield(t).

So:
- If yield returns true (caller wants to continue), !yield(t) is false, Each continues
- If yield returns false (caller wants to stop), !yield(t) is true, Each stops

This seems correct.

But perhaps there's an issue with the semantics. Let me re-read the test to understand what might be wrong.

Actually, let me just make a simple change to make the logic more explicit and see if that fixes any potential issue:

```bash
sed -i '/return !yield(t)/c\\t\t\tcontinueIteration := yield(t)\n\t\t\treturn !continueIteration' set.go
```

